A||||3||Sevaan||Need help!..again...||||9||
Z||000000||Sevaan||03-21-2001||07:58 PM||||If it's not one thing, it's another!<P>So, the engine runs fine until I come to a stop. Then I can feel the thing vibrate and it idles from 750 to 1000, jumping back and forth constantly.<P>chug chug chug    chuug   chuug   chuug   chug chug chug    chuug   chuug   chuug   chug chug chug ...etc.<P>And when I'm come to a full stop, the whole car stalls.<P>I drive a 1986 automatic coupe, btw.<P>Now, I've found that I can slip it into neutral when I come to a stop. It still idles the same way but it doesn't stall. Only if I'm in gear will it do that.<P>It's quite bothersome. I've stalled in a couple intersections.<P>What is the problem, and how can I fix it?||||reg||9
Z||000001||skitime||03-21-2001||09:15 PM||||I don't know if you have a 4 or 6 cylinder but I just fixed a 86 V6 GT with the same problem.  I replaced the EGR valve and it was a new car. No more stalling and it idles better too. <P>Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||03-21-2001||09:33 PM||||Well, i dont know that much about automatics, but could is possibly be that the torque converter is staying locked, causing more force being transmitted to the wheels, and when you come to a stop the wheels are held by the brakes, so it works against your engine and then it dies because of that?<P>EGR is also a good canidate, if it is staying open and letting more exhaust gas into the air/fuel mixture, the mixture wont be rich enough to keep the engine going under the load of the brakes working against it.<P>The EGR valve is located on the trunk side of the rocker cover (I assume this is a 4cyl coupe), sitting on top of the intake manifold.  When the car is running, reach under and stick your finger in one of the holes on the underside of the EGR valve, until you can feel the diaphragm.  While lightly feeling the diaphragm, rev the engine and see if the valve moves up with higher RPM's.  It works on vacuum, more revs makes more vacuum and pulls the diaphragm open more.  If it doesnt move, it is definatly bad.  About $50 for new one.<P>It is best to do this test on a fairly cool engine, the valve is in direct contact with hot exhaust gases.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fierowrecker||03-21-2001||09:38 PM||||If you think it is the EGR, remove the valve and see if you can blow air by the valve (use a hose...).<BR>If you can, TAP the pintle (the part that sticks out of the tube) with a hammer, and try the leak check again.<BR>If it still leaks, replace it, if it does not, reinstall it, but leave the vac hose off...<BR>If it is a Duke, make a block off plate and cover the holes, use a new gasket as a template, and make it .25 inch thick...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
